    "body": "### Problem\r\n\r\n`vim_rename()` removes the destination before its first rename attempt. If the process stops between those operations, the previous destination is lost, including when replacing a ShaDa file.\r\n\r\n### Solution\r\n\r\nTry `os_rename()` before removing the destination. If that fails and removing the destination succeeds, retry the rename before the existing copy fallback. This preserves directory-to-existing-file behavior.\r\n\r\nThis avoids the deletion gap when the initial rename succeeds. The remove/copy fallback can still lose the destination if a later operation fails; this change does not provide general crash safety or change ShaDa fsync ordering.\r\n\r\nFixes #41940.\r\n\r\n### Validation\r\n\r\n- Directory-to-existing-file regression fails on the original PR head (`1dda754a7b`) and passes after the correction\r\n- Core fileio functional suite: 13 passed\r\n- Changed-file C lint and whitespace checks pass\r\n\r\nLocal validation is Linux-only. Functional tests use stdio RPC with only the optional test-ID listener suppressed. Crash injection was unavailable because ptrace is blocked.\r\n\r\nAI-assisted.",
    "closed_at": "2026-10-03T00:27:04Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This has issues, and is a good example of why asking your AI to *review its own changes* is very important.\r\n\r\nAI findings:\r\n\r\n**Regression:** renaming a directory onto an existing file now fails *and* deletes the file. `rename('Xdir', 'Xfile')` used to return 0; now it returns -1 and `Xfile` is gone (reproduced). `os_rename` fails with ENOTDIR, `os_remove(to)`\r\ndeletes the file, and `vim_copyfile` can't copy a directory. Users can hit this through netrw `R`.\r\n→ Suggested fix: when `os_remove(to)` succeeds, retry `os_rename` before falling back to the copy. That also covers Windows, where `MoveFileExW` fails if `to` is read-only or open in another process, so those cases now go through remove\r\nplus a full copy.\r\n\r\n**The fallback still deletes `to` first,** on any rename failure (not only EXDEV), before a copy that can fail (ENOSPC, EACCES, a crash mid-copy). So \"the destination was already destroyed\" is not fixed on that path. A deeper fix: copy\r\nto a temp file next to `to`, then `os_rename` it into place.\r\n\r\n**Tests:**\r\n- The new test passes without the fix.\r\n- Replacing an existing file is already covered by `Test_rename_file_to_file` and `fs_spec.lua:457`.\r\n- Add the regression case to `Test_rename_dir_to_dir` instead; it fails with this PR:\r\n  ```vim\r\n  call writefile(['x'], 'Xrenamefile')\r\n  call assert_equal(0, rename('Xrenamedir1', 'Xrenamefile'))\r\n  call assert_true(isdirectory('Xrenamefile'))\r\n  ```\r\n\r\n**Nits:** \"atomically replaces `to`\" isn't true in the cases above. The old \"Rename() failed, try copying the file.\" comment now repeats the new one.\r\n\r\n**Pre-existing:**\r\n- `shada.c:2859` renames before the fsync at :2882, so a power loss can still truncate `main.shada`.\r\n- Renaming between two hard links to the same file returns 0 and leaves a stray temp file (`rename_with_tmp`).\r\n",
